  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nMonday: Shower Chances Return\n\nAs we head into the day on Monday, an upper-level trough/cut-off low \nwill eject into the region increasing potential for shower chances \nduring this period. Overall, highest confidence for seeing any \nprecipitation potential generally remains south of I-90 given the \nposition of this upper-level low and where the CAMs largely paint \nprecipitation during the afternoon and evening on Monday. \nConsequently, much of the higher probabilities (50-80%) in the \nHREF for measurable precipitation remain largely south of I-90 \nduring this timeframe. Additionally, with the overall weaker \nQ-vector convergence during this timeframe being the primary \nforcing regime, probabilities in the HREF for rainfall totals \nover 1 inch are very low (under 10%). \n\nTuesday - Friday: Staying Cool and Drier\n\nBy the time we get past Monday, northeasterly surface flow and high \npressure south of the Hudson Bay will allow for cooler and \ndrier conditions to advect into our region. As a result, median \nhigh temperatures in the NBM throughout the week generally \nremain in the 60s for most of the local area. As we head into \nWednesday morning, upper-level ridging will push into the local \narea which in turn should both allow for a gradual warming trend\nand clearing skies during this period. Consequently, not seeing\nmuch for precipitation chances till later into the week when \nmaybe some shortwave perturbations try to sneak back into the \nregion during the Thursday and Friday timeframe. However, \npredictability with these waves remains very low at this time. \nIn any case, will have to watch Wednesday morning for some frost\npotential across north-central WI with median low temperatures \nin the middle to upper 30s.",
